{% extends "pages/_example.jinja" %}
{% set example_slug = 'first-command' %}
{% set example_facts = [
  ('Level', 'Beginner'),
  ('Needs', 'Python ' ~ python_floor ~ ' or later, and wc on PATH (any POSIX system)'),
  ('Install', 'Cuprum from main@' ~ cuprum_ref ~ '; see Getting started'),
  ('Tested', cuprum_verified_on ~ ' against main@' ~ cuprum_ref ~ ' on Linux, Python ' ~ python_floor),
  ('Uses', 'ProgramCatalogue.from_programs, sh.make, StdinInput, scoped(catalogue=…)'),
] %}

{% block problem %}
<p>
  A script needs one external tool — here, <code>wc</code>, to count the words
  in a piece of text. With <code>subprocess</code> that is a string or a list
  assembled wherever it is needed, run with whatever is on the machine. With
  Cuprum the program is declared once in a catalogue, the command is built
  from typed arguments, and the script says exactly which programs it may run.
</p>
<p>
  This job builds that catalogue, feeds the text on standard input rather
  than as an argument, and turns the captured output into an integer.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block code %}
{% call ui.code_panel('word_count.py', 'Complete', foot=[verified]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
from cuprum import (
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    StdinInput,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

WC = Program("wc")
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(WC, name="word-count")

count_words = sh.make(WC, catalogue=CATALOGUE)


def word_count(text: str) -> int:
    cmd = count_words("-w")
    with scoped(catalogue=CATALOGUE):
        result = cmd.run_sync(stdin=StdinInput(text=text))
    if not result.ok:
        msg = f"wc failed with exit code {result.exit_code}: {result.stderr}"
        raise RuntimeError(msg)
    return int((result.stdout or "").strip())


print(count_words("-w").argv_with_program)
print(count_words("-w").project.name)
print(word_count("Every command gets the right fitting."))
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
<div class="cu-prose mt-10">
  <h3>Reading it</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(WC, name="word-count")</code> builds
      a single-project catalogue whose allowlist is exactly <code>wc</code>.
      Without <code>name=</code>, the project would be named after the
      programs' base names.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sh.make(WC, catalogue=CATALOGUE)</code> returns a builder. It has
      already checked that <code>wc</code> is catalogued, so every command it
      builds carries the project's metadata.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>StdinInput(text=...)</code> is encoded with the context's encoding
      (UTF-8 by default) and written to the child's standard input, so the
      text never appears in argv.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>strip()</code> is there because <code>wc</code> pads its count
      differently on different systems. Capture keeps the child's bytes as
      they were.
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block output %}
<div class="cu-pair">
  {% call ui.output('Expected output', 'stdout') %}
('wc', '-w')
word-count
6
  {% endcall %}
  <div class="cu-prose">
    <p>
      The first line is the exact argv Cuprum will pass — program first, no
      shell. The second is the project the command belongs to, which is what
      downstream tooling reads to find noise rules and documentation links.
      The third is the count.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block failure %}
<div class="cu-prose">
  <p>
    Cuprum refuses a program at two different moments, for two different
    reasons. This script triggers both.
  </p>
</div>
<div class="cu-pair mt-8">
  {% call ui.code_panel('refusals.py', 'Failure path', foot=[verified], flat=true) %}
  {% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
from cuprum import (
    ECHO,
    ForbiddenProgramError,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    UnknownProgramError,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

WC = Program("wc")
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(WC, name="word-count")

# Not in the catalogue: refused when the builder is made.
try:
    sh.make(Program("curl"), catalogue=CATALOGUE)
except UnknownProgramError as exc:
    print(f"UnknownProgramError: {exc}")

# In a catalogue, but outside the active scope: refused when it runs.
with scoped(catalogue=CATALOGUE):
    try:
        sh.make(ECHO)("hello").run_sync()
    except ForbiddenProgramError as exc:
        print(f"ForbiddenProgramError: {exc}")
  {% endhighlight %}
  {% endcall %}
  <div>
    {% call ui.output('Expected output', 'stdout') %}
UnknownProgramError: Program 'curl' is not in the catalogue allowlist
ForbiddenProgramError: Program 'echo' is not allowed in the current context
    {% endcall %}
    {% call ui.output('Also expected', 'stderr, logger cuprum.context') %}
Program echo denied by context allowlist restricted_state=True
    {% endcall %}
  </div>
</div>
<div class="cu-prose mt-10">
  <p>
    <code>UnknownProgramError</code> is a construction failure: the catalogue
    you passed does not know the program, so no command exists to run.
    <code>ForbiddenProgramError</code> is an authorization failure:
    <code>ECHO</code> is in Cuprum's default catalogue, so the command was
    built, but the scope around it allows only <code>wc</code>. Nothing is
    spawned in either case.
  </p>
  <p>
    A non-zero exit is not an exception. If <code>wc</code> itself failed,
    <code>result.ok</code> would be false and the script above raises its own
    <code>RuntimeError</code> with the exit code and stderr.
  </p>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block policy %}
<p>
  Two independent checks let this run. The builder was made from a catalogue
  that lists <code>wc</code>, and the <code>scoped()</code> block takes its
  allowlist from that same catalogue, so <code>wc</code> is the one program
  the block may run.
</p>
<p>
  The scope is what a reviewer reads. Remove the <code>with</code> block and
  the command still runs, because an unscoped root context permits every
  catalogued program; that default exists to ease adoption, and narrowing it
  is how a script states its policy.
</p>
{% endblock %}
